{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BT9wpyjNSM97itVgZ3veSr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DNZcwBpqLvnzph9yp8CA9A","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment: R1 — Methods & Experiments\n\n**Paper:** \"Psychometric Detection and Regeneration of Contaminated LLM Benchmark Items: Differential Item Functioning at Scale\"\n\n**Your focus:** Methods rigor, experimental validity, statistical soundness, and results interpretation.\n\n**Venue standard:** Top ML venue (e.g., ICLR/NeurIPS), acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept).\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nThe paper reframes LLM benchmark contamination as a psychometric problem — contaminated items exhibit Differential Item Functioning (DIF), becoming differentially easier for exposed model cohorts beyond ability predictions. Key contributions: (1) first large-scale IRT fit diagnostics for LLM populations (5,219 models × 12,508 MMLU items), showing architecture heterogeneity is the primary obstacle and decoder-only stratification yields acceptable fit (RMSEA 0.068); (2) DIF+ConStat dual-signal ensemble (AUROC 0.72); (3) semi-synthetic validation framework revealing simulation-to-real gap (TPR drops 0.15); (4) pilot-scale bifactor IRT equating for benchmark regeneration (ρ=0.91 on holdout); (5) three-parameter power surface (R²=0.89) characterizing detection boundaries.\n\n### Key Experimental Results to Scrutinize\n\n**IRT Fit (Table 1):** Full-pop RMSEA=0.105 (poor), decoder-only=0.068 (acceptable), bifactor=0.062. Architecture stratification resolves the heterogeneity problem.\n\n**DIF Detection (Tables 3-4):** 485 ETS-C flags on temporal cohort. Within-family analysis shows 42% overlap — nearly half of DIF flags may reflect capability growth rather than contamination. MMLU-CF validation weak (κ=0.14, r=0.18).\n\n**RL Robustness (Tables 5-6):** Alignment-RL robust (N=477, retention 0.88, DeLong p=0.008). Reasoning-RL only 13 models — \"preliminary evidence\" framing. Per-model detectors (ConStat, Min-K%) degrade to ~0.50 AUROC on reasoning-RL while DIF maintains 0.79.\n\n**ConStat (Table 8):** AUROC 0.67 (non-RL), 0.52 (reasoning-RL). 38% overlap with DIF flags.\n\n**DVD (Table 9):** AUROC 0.56 overall, excluded from primary ensemble. Reported as negative result.\n\n**Ensemble (Table 10):** DIF+ConStat union AUROC 0.72, TPR 0.58 at δ≥0.8. Adding DVD degrades (AUROC 0.70, FPR increases). Coverage gap at reasoning-RL + medium effects.\n\n**Semi-synthetic (Tables 13-15):** TPR=0.58 at δ≥1.4 (vs 0.73 in simulation). Power surface R²=0.89 — exploitation rate is strongest determinant.\n\n**Cross-benchmark GSM8K (Table 12):** r=0.36 (p=0.004) with GSM1K gap. Corroborating but not conclusive.\n\n**Bifactor + Regeneration (Tables 16-18):** Student simulation ρ=0.78 >> prompt-based 0.28. Holdout ρ=0.91 but systematic quality degradation (factor loading 0.41 vs 0.50, discrimination 0.95 vs 1.18). Critical replacement threshold 28%.\n\n### Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nThe review_context.yaml lists 11 imagined experiments. The paper covers all of them:\n- plan_001 (real data DIF MMLU) → §4.1-4.2 ✓\n- plan_002 (RL robustness) → §4.3 ✓\n- plan_003a (ConStat) → §4.4 ✓\n- plan_003b (DVD) → §4.5 ✓\n- plan_003c (ensemble fusion) → §4.6 ✓\n- plan_004 (GSM8K cross-benchmark) → §4.7 ✓\n- plan_005 (bifactor equating) → §4.9 ✓\n- plan_006 (item generation) → §4.9 ✓\n- plan_007 (cumulative Bayesian DIF) → §5.2 ✓\n- plan_008 (graceful degradation) → §4.10 ✓\n- plan_009 (item-focused trees) → §5.1 ✓\n- plan_010 (architecture stratified) → §4.1 ✓\n- plan_011 (semi-synthetic) → §4.8 ✓\n\nAll experiments covered, some at abbreviated depth in supplementary sections.\n\n### Questions for Your Review\n\n1. Is the 2PL IRT model appropriate for this population? Are there violations beyond architecture heterogeneity (e.g., local dependence across MMLU items within subjects)?\n2. Is the Mantel-Haenszel DIF procedure with ETS classification well-justified? Are there more powerful alternatives that should have been considered?\n3. The semi-synthetic validation is the primary ground-truth mechanism — is the injection protocol sound? Does injecting contamination into a matrix that may already contain contamination bias results?\n4. The 42% within-family overlap is presented as a limitation, but could it undermine the entire DIF approach? How should this be interpreted?\n5. With only 13 reasoning-RL models, is the \"preliminary evidence\" framing sufficient, or is this a critical gap that undermines the paper's timeliness?\n6. The ensemble AUROC of 0.72 — is this competitive enough as a standalone contribution?\n7. Student simulation calibration (ρ=0.78) — is this a meaningful contribution or incremental over Li et al. (2025)?\n8. Are the statistical tests appropriate (DeLong, TOST, bootstrap CI)?\n\nProvide a structured review with: Summary, Strengths (numbered), Weaknesses (numbered with severity: major/minor), Questions for Authors, Missing Experiments/Analyses, and a preliminary score (1-6).","reviewer_id":"1","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7699,"cache_read_input_tokens":49669,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7699},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b46c9273-8153-430c-87ab-61a378504351","uuid":"9c987b58-f7a2-4b9b-bbfe-81c5e1a669a9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DNZcwBpqLvnzph9yp8CA9A","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_f025582a920d3f79beb59dfc17929017:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b46c9273-8153-430c-87ab-61a378504351","uuid":"c5aac3b0-1c22-486b-91d0-c82bd4f340c0","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:35:43.180Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_f025582a920d3f79beb59dfc17929017:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_f025582a920d3f79beb59dfc17929017:reviewer_1\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BT9wpyjNSM97itVgZ3veSr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LKibsw7UMyBG7sDgeeVCYV","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## Review Assignment: R2 — Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Paper:** \"Psychometric Detection and Regeneration of Contaminated LLM Benchmark Items: Differential Item Functioning at Scale\"\n\n**Your focus:** Novelty assessment, positioning relative to prior work, framing quality, and broader impact/significance.\n\n**Venue standard:** Top ML venue (e.g., ICLR/NeurIPS), acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept).\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nThe paper reframes LLM benchmark contamination as a psychometric problem using Differential Item Functioning (DIF). Key contributions claimed: (1) first large-scale IRT fit diagnostics for LLM populations revealing architecture heterogeneity as primary obstacle; (2) DIF+ConStat dual-signal ensemble (AUROC 0.72) with RL robustness; (3) semi-synthetic validation framework; (4) pilot-scale bifactor IRT equating for benchmark regeneration (ρ=0.91); (5) three-parameter detection power surface.\n\n### Related Work Cited\n\n1. Wang et al. (2026) — contamination signals vanish after RL (ICLR 2026)\n2. White et al. (2024) — LiveBench\n3. Oren et al. (2024) — ConStat (NeurIPS 2024)\n4. Zhang et al. (2026) — Growing Pains: multidimensional IRT for LLM evaluation\n5. Perlitz et al. (2025) — METABENCH (ICLR 2025)\n6. Holland & Thayer (1988) — DIF and Mantel-Haenszel procedure\n7. Li et al. (2025) — Student simulation for item difficulty estimation\n8. Zhang et al. (2024) — GSM8K examination\n9. Wiegreffe et al. (2024) — MMLU-CF\n10. Dunn et al. (2024) — DVD generation variance detection\n\n### Questions for Your Review\n\n1. **Novelty of DIF for contamination detection:** DIF is a well-established psychometric technique (Holland & Thayer, 1988). How novel is its application to LLM contamination? Is this a genuine conceptual contribution or a straightforward application of existing methodology?\n\n2. **Relationship to Growing Pains (Zhang et al., 2026):** Growing Pains also uses multidimensional IRT for LLM evaluation. How does this paper differentiate? Is the bifactor modeling contribution incremental given Growing Pains?\n\n3. **Relationship to ConStat (Oren et al., 2024):** The paper uses ConStat as a component. Is the ensemble combination (DIF+ConStat) novel or is it a simple union of existing methods?\n\n4. **Relationship to Wang et al. (2026):** The RL robustness argument is central. Is this paper's contribution adequately differentiated from Wang et al.'s findings about RL degrading existing detectors?\n\n5. **Framing assessment:** The paper frames contributions as \"methodological rather than systemic\" (Discussion §6). Is this honest positioning or underselling/overselling?\n\n6. **Missing related work:** \n   - Are there other psychometric approaches to LLM evaluation not cited?\n   - Benchmark contamination detection literature beyond the cited works (e.g., n-gram overlap methods, membership inference)?\n   - IRT applications in NLP/AI fairness contexts?\n   - Other benchmark regeneration/dynamic benchmark approaches?\n\n7. **Significance of negative results:** DVD negative result (AUROC 0.56), MMLU-CF low reliability (κ=0.14). Are these valuable contributions to the community?\n\n8. **Boundary conditions framing:** The paper explicitly states boundary conditions (architecture homogeneity, large effects only, reasoning-RL gap, 28% replacement threshold). Is this level of qualification appropriate for a top venue, or does it undermine the contributions?\n\n9. **Writing quality:** Abstract density, claim calibration, honesty of limitations presentation.\n\n10. **Broader impact:** Does this work change how the community should think about contamination? Is there practical value for benchmark maintainers?\n\n### Key Numbers for Calibration\n\n- DIF alone: AUROC 0.76, TPR 0.73 at δ≥1.4\n- Ensemble: AUROC 0.72 (paradoxically lower than DIF alone because ConStat adds FPR)\n- Semi-synthetic TPR: 0.58 (vs 0.73 in simulation) — 15-point gap\n- Regeneration: ρ=0.91 holdout, but systematic quality degradation\n- Within-family confound: 42% overlap\n- Reasoning-RL: only 13 models tested\n- GSM8K cross-validation: r=0.36 (moderate)\n\nProvide a structured review with: Summary, Strengths (numbered), Weaknesses (numbered with severity: major/minor), Questions for Authors, Missing Related Work, and a preliminary score (1-6).","reviewer_id":"2","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7699,"cache_read_input_tokens":49669,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7699},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b46c9273-8153-430c-87ab-61a378504351","uuid":"cc7e0a6b-e8e5-496d-aa14-0fe0a5b1d0f9"}
